r_i ≤ x_i, Σr = E}, where x_i = min(v_i, V − v_P) and E = v_P − Σp^VCG. The minimum revenue R is v_P.\n2. The Vickrey-nearest point is the point of F closest to r = 0. If it differs from the plain projection (E/3)(1,1,1), it lies on the edge of F. The centroid of a 2-D polygon is always strictly inside, so the two points can only match when the centroid equals (E/3)(1,1,1).\n3. F is the full triangle with separate corner triangles cut off. Working out the centroid gives the condition that the three numbers ((E−x_i)⁺)²·x_i are all equal.\n4. Solving that equation gives exactly cases (a)–(c).\n\n**Example where they differ:** v_P = 6, v = (1, 3, 4). VCG payments are (0, 1, 2). The Vickrey-nearest point is (1, 2, 3), but the centroid is (5/9, 20/9, 29/9). The answer also gives one example each for (a) and (b) where the points coincide.\n\n**Checks:** a brute-force comparison over all integer values from 1 to 8 (6,400 cases) found no case where the condition and the actual comparison disagree. Random real-valued tests showed a few mismatches, but only very near the boundary of the condition, where the two points differ by less than the numerical tolerance.","ttft_ms":2309,"type":"result","duration_ms":431591,"uuid":"57336d86-1c3b-420d-80ea-23ceceb5a033","ttft_stream_ms":1917,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":1917,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
